#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167990 :tutorial/content "Consider an **inhomogeneous** linear system of ordinary differential equations in component form:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={a}_{11}{x}_{1}(t)+{a}_{12}{x}_{2}(t)+{f}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={a}_{21}{x}_{1}(t)+{a}_{22}{x}_{2}(t)+{f}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we can package the unknown functions into vectors\n![](images/03/037289f6f3a779d750cef23dc18d149786604bd477565e3708d6470f2e3c2f7a.svg)\n\nThen, the system can be written as\n![](images/77/77fc846681e1f0a98675b06f962e42f4491fd07d9bb2eab291e69cd49e4dd5f9.svg)\n\nOften, the **forcing functions** $f_1(t), f_2(t)$ are linear combinations of a few basic functions (such as $e^{3t},$ $t^2,$ or $\\sin t$).\nIn that case, it is useful to write $\\mathbf{g}(t)$ as a sum of those basic functions times constant vectors.\n\nFor example, let's write down the system of linear ODEs below in matrix form.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)+8{e}^{3t} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-3{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)+10{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{3t}\\cdot 8+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=(-3)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{3t}\\cdot 0+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n![](images/47/47fb751641455ab4cf85756166fa8811bd8fe3ddc6bcb6005c8e6be8c31b6fe1.svg)\nwhere ![](images/f4/f47e1272123fb8f4a5f54931929b9492f54e9d78d590b2ee28394bf29976ce44.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "02676de3-99b6-59c3-bb19-fb44e8c8dda4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14390 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168018 :tutorial/content "Recall that an inhomogeneous linear system has the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant matrix and $\\mathbf{g}(t)$ is a known forcing term. The associated *homogeneous* system is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t).\n$$\n\nA *general solution* of the *homogeneous* system is called the **complementary solution** and is denoted by $\\mathbf{x}_c(t)$.\nA **particular solution** is any single function $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ that satisfies the full inhomogeneous system.\n\nThese two pieces combine to give the *general solution* of the inhomogeneous system:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=\\mathbf{x}_c(t)+\\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nIndeed, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{c}+{x}_{p})^{′} & ={x}_{c}^{′}+{x}_{p}^{′} \\\\  & =(A{x}_{c})+(A{x}_{p}+g(t)) \\\\  & =A{x}_{c}+A{x}_{p}+g(t) \\\\  & =A({x}_{c}+{x}_{p})+g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\mathbf{x}_c+\\mathbf{x}_p)' = A(\\mathbf{x}_c+\\mathbf{x}_p)+\\mathbf{g}(t)$, the sum is a solution of the inhomogeneous system.\n\nNotice that it doesn't matter which particular solution we take.\nSuppose we found two different particular solutions, $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ and $\\mathbf{y}_p(t).$ Let's examine their difference, $\\mathbf{w}(t) = \\mathbf{y}_p(t) - \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$.\n\nBy definition, we know that $\\mathbf{y}_p' = A\\mathbf{y}_p + \\mathbf{g}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_p' = A\\mathbf{x}_p + \\mathbf{g}(t).$\nNow, let's find the derivative of the difference $\\mathbf{w}(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}^{′} & =({y}_{p}-{x}_{p})^{′} \\\\  & ={y}_{p}^{′}-{x}_{p}^{′} \\\\  & =(A{y}_{p}+g(t))-(A{x}_{p}+g(t)) \\\\  & =A{y}_{p}-A{x}_{p} \\\\  & =A({y}_{p}-{x}_{p}) \\\\  & =Aw\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result $\\mathbf{w}' = A\\mathbf{w}$ means that the difference between any two particular solutions is itself a solution to the *homogeneous* system.\n\nThis is why *any* particular solution is enough. Any other particular solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}_{p} = \\underbrace{(\\mathbf{y}_{p}-\\mathbf{x}_{p})}_{\\text{homogeneous}} \\:\\:\\: + \\underbrace{\\mathbf{x}_{p}}_{\\text{particular}} \\!\\!\\!\\!\\!.\n$$\n\nThe \"variety\" among particular solutions is already captured by the complementary solution $\\mathbf{x}_c(t)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "07b5883c-d1bc-5616-8d06-61b24dc79288" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14560 :tutorial/title "Complementary vs. Particular Solutions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168019 :tutorial/content "To solve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t),\n$$\n\nwe often look for a particular solution $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ whose *form matches the forcing term* $\\mathbf{g}(t)$.\n\nIn each case below, the unknowns are constant vectors (to be determined by substitution).\n\n- **Constant forcing.** If $\\mathbf{g}(t)$ is a constant vector, then we try\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t)=\\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{a}$ is a constant vector.\n\n- **Polynomial forcing.** If $\\mathbf{g}(t)$ is a polynomial of degree $n$, then we try\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \\mathbf{a}_n t^n+\\mathbf{a}_{n-1} t^{n-1}+\\cdots+\\mathbf{a}_1 t+\\mathbf{a}_0\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{a}_n,\\mathbf{a}_{n-1},\\ldots,\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_0$ are constant vectors.\n\n- **Exponential forcing.** If $\\mathbf{g}(t)=e^{\\alpha t}\\mathbf{v}$ for some constant vector $\\mathbf{v},$ then we try\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t)=e^{\\alpha t}\\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{a}$ is a constant vector.\n\n- **Trigonometric forcing.** If $\\mathbf{g}(t)=\\cos(\\beta t)\\mathbf{v}$ or $\\mathbf{g}(t)=\\sin(\\beta t)\\mathbf{v}$ for some constant vector $\\mathbf{v},$ then we try\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t)=\\cos(\\beta t)\\mathbf{a}+\\sin(\\beta t)\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are constant vectors.\n\nIn the next step, we substitute the chosen $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ into the differential equation to solve for the unknown vectors.\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "799084bf-9121-5217-8f53-70b9e09e61d2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14561 :tutorial/title "Choosing the Form of a Particular Solution"}]}
